   KOREA, AUG 25-29, 2024},
Abstract = {Burnout is an occupational condition of concern for health care workers,
   in particular nurses. Wearable sensors may be useful components of
   programs to monitor and improve burnout, but these devices should be
   usable. Prior experience with similar devices and length of exposure can
   influence perceptions about usability. We investigated the relationship
   between prior experience with and length of exposure to wristbands with
   perceived usability. Eighteen nurses from two hospitals wore the
   Empatica E4 wristband during working shifts for four weeks. Afterwards,
   they completed a survey including two yes/no questions regarding their
   experience and perceptions towards wristbands and the System Usability
   Scale (SUS). The Empatica E4 received an average SUS score of 85.14.
   Participants who wore wristbands before the study reported higher SUS
   scores, but participants who wore the Empatica E4 wristband longer in
   total, across more shifts and on shorter shifts reported lower SUS
   scores. Usability evaluations may more accurate when a longitudinal
   evaluation design is used.},
